"Mistral raises €3B to make sovereign, open-weight AI the technology frontier European AI company Mistral has raised €3 billion in a Series D funding round to strengthen open-weight sovereign AI capabilities and expand its global infrastructure. Mistral raised €3 billion in a Series D round led by Samsung Electronics, with participation from Scaleup Europe Fund, PSG Equity, and others, surpassing a valuation of €21 billion. The newly secured capital will be deployed to expand computing power for frontier AI research and model training, as well as to build global infrastructure and accelerate commercialization. Through a full stack of sovereign AI encompassing internal data storage, controllable models, and independent computing environments, it supports enterprise mission-critical AI transformation without vendor lock-in. Hallo, Deutschland! Mistral AI has opened a new hub in Munich, Germany, aiming to strengthen Physics and Industrial AI capabilities for Europe's largest manufacturing and industrial enterprises while building European AI sovereignty. Mistral establishes a new technology hub in Munich, stationing field engineers and specialized research teams dedicated to Physics AI and Industrial AI. Leveraging talent from Emmi AI acquired in May 2026, the company is conducting real-world collaborative proof-of-concepts with BMW for crash simulations and Siemens Energy for Industrial AI. To secure European AI sovereignty, it plans to deploy 1 gigawatt (GW) of compute capacity by 2030 and provide a secure enterprise environment based on open-weight architecture. Mistral and Mozilla are bringing open, private and multilingual AI to your web browser Mistral and Mozilla are collaborating to bring Mistral's open-weight language models to Firefox's AI browsing assistant, 'Firefox Smart Window'. Powered by Mistral models, Firefox Smart Window (beta) supports tab-based information exploration, organizing complex searches, and remembering previous browsing content. It will first be available to users in France and North America, with support expanding to the UK and Germany later this year. Privacy protections such as not saving conversations on Mozilla servers and Mistral's zero data retention policy are applied, alongside fine-tuning that reflects regional languages, dialects, and cultural nuances. Cloudera and Mistral Partner to Bring Specialized, Sovereign Intelligence to Enterprise Data Mistral and Cloudera have partnered to empower enterprises to build and operate customized AI models while maintaining data sovereignty. Mistral's AI models integrate with Cloudera's hybrid data platform to support running inference on-premises, across public/private clouds, and in completely air-gapped environments. Enterprises can leverage their proprietary data within controlled environments to train custom AI models, maintaining full ownership over both their data and the resulting intelligence. Addressing the demand for sovereign AI across regulated industries (such as finance, manufacturing, and telecommunications), Mistral AI's capabilities are combined with the 30 exabytes of customer data managed on Cloudera's platform. Modernizing complex legacy code with AI agents. Introduces a case study where Mistral successfully migrated a complex 40,000-line Fortran 77 legacy codebase to modern C++ for a European energy company using AI agents and structured workflows. Modernized a 40,000-line Fortran 77 reservoir simulator into C++ through a structured workflow that combined building an equivalence harness for mathematical verification, codebase documentation via AI agents, and human reviews. Transitioning from a procedural language to object-oriented C++ involves architectural refactoring, and constraints of Fortran 77—such as global memory (COMMON blocks) and implicit typing—make a verification framework beyond mere syntax translation essential. Key factors for successful legacy modernization include prioritizing the verification of numerical alignment in final outputs and critical intermediate checkpoints, organizing documentation prior to migration, and maintaining a balance between agent autonomy and engineer oversight. From Scan to Treatment Plan, AI Helps Close Breast Cancer’s Deadliest Gaps Startups participating in the NVIDIA Inception program are developing AI-based solutions to bridge gaps in healthcare from breast cancer screening to treatment planning. A shortage of radiologists in the United States and time-consuming genetic testing are creating bottlenecks in early diagnosis and prompt treatment decisions for breast cancer. iSono Health, through its FDA-cleared wearable automated 3D ultrasound platform 'ATUSA', reduced scan times from up to 45 minutes to about 2 minutes per breast while improving sensitivity by 28%. Standardized automated exams minimize operator error and enable year-over-year comparative analysis, with current multi-site clinical studies underway to expand into multimodal diagnostic intelligence. North 2: Enterprise AI without compromises Cohere has unveiled 'North 2', an agentic AI platform that significantly enhances orchestration and governance capabilities and supports diverse deployment options for enterprise environments. Introduces a redesigned orchestration harness supporting complex multi-step task automation and agent composition. Supports a model-agnostic environment compatible with external models in addition to Cohere's own models, along with flexible deployment across cloud, on-premises, and air-gapped environments. Provides integration with various enterprise tools such as Slack, SharePoint, Jira, and GitHub, as well as reusable skills, memory, and governance features for detailed cost and usage control. Researchers are tracking a Chinese AI ‘agent fleet’ Independent researchers are tracking a Chinese AI agent fleet running on Tencent infrastructure and conducting parallel activities targeting Alibaba's mapping service. Researchers analyzed that it performs multiple parallel tasks in the form of an 'agent fleet' rather than a 'swarm,' as there are no traces of inter-agent communication. Traffic monitoring through the domain scanning service URLquery captured records of the agents sending direction queries for entrances to public places such as parks, zoos, and hospitals. To date, it appears to be bypassing Alibaba's API terms, but unidentified, unauthorized AI agent activity has been continuously increasing across the internet recently. Meet the Startup Battlefield 200 judges who’ll decide the winner at TechCrunch Disrupt 2026 Introduces the key lineup of judges who will decide the final winner at the Startup Battlefield 200 finals of TechCrunch Disrupt 2026. Out of thousands of startups, 200 were selected, and 20 of them will pitch on stage from October 13 to 15, 2026. The finals judging panel consists of five veteran investors who have either founded or co-founded their own investment firms. The career backgrounds and investment portfolios of key judges—including Stacy Brown-Philpot of Cherryrock Capital, Chi-Hua Chien of Goodwater Capital, Dayna Grayson of Construct Capital, and Carter Reum of M13—were revealed. Can Safeworld convince people that GenAI robots won’t hurt them? Researchers from Carnegie Mellon University founded Safeworld, a startup developing a simulation evaluation platform to verify the safety of generative AI-powered humanoid robots, and secured $12 million in seed funding. Safeworld was established to assess the risks of and build trust in generative AI-controlled robots, which are difficult to predict unlike those running traditional algorithms. Safeworld pre-verifies robot safety and collision avoidance by simulating real human models and unstructured environments within physics simulation engines such as Genesis and MuJoCo. Led by Shine Capital and a16z Speedrun, the company raised more than $12 million in seed funding to launch its full-scale operations. An open-source tool lets you delete 12GB of Apple Intelligence data on macOS An open-source tool called 'RemoveMacAI' has been released, which disables Apple Intelligence features on macOS and frees up storage space by deleting more than 12GB of AI model data. It was developed to solve the issue in macOS 27 where the master toggle to disable Apple Intelligence disappeared and model data remained on disk. It turns off Siri, Writing Tools, Genmoji, Image Playground, ChatGPT extensions, and more all at once, deletes the models, and blocks re-downloading. Changes persist across macOS updates, settings can be restored using the 'removemacai revert' command, and dictation functionality remains fully operational. OpenAI is sticking more ads in ChatGPT OpenAI is introducing a new visual ad format in ChatGPT that displays sponsored products and services during image generation, beginning testing in the US. OpenAI will test a visual ad format later this month in the US that displays images of sponsored products and services on screen when generating images in ChatGPT. The new visual ads are designed to remain separate from generated images and will not influence the content of ChatGPT's answers. Ads will not appear for paid subscribers such as Plus, Pro, and Enterprise, and safeguards will be applied to block ads in emotionally vulnerable or sensitive contexts. Our minds aren’t equipped to handle AI This article refutes the AI industry's view of treating the human brain as a mere computer algorithm, arguing instead that the brain should be understood as an evolutionary feedback control system. The AI industry views the brain as a simple information processing device—such as a 'biological Turing machine' or a 'biological computer'—but this perspective overlooks the complexity of human nature. The computationalist model, which reduces cognitive processes to a three-step algorithm of input-computation-output, fails to reflect the actual evolutionary history of the brain. Paul Cisek, a neuroscientist at the University of Montreal, argues that the brain should be viewed not simply as an information processor, but as a feedback control system that actively regulates input through interaction with the environment. Yandex Introduces Sona: A Single Generative Recommender That Replaces Entire Recommendation Cascade Yandex announced 'Sona', a unified recommendation architecture that replaces multi-stage recommendation system pipelines with a single generative transformer model. Consolidated a cascade structure previously split into over 15 models—including candidate generation, pre-ranking, and heavy ranking—into a single model. Eliminated manual feature engineering and shared a single user representation by utilizing log events and 3-code-based Semantic IDs. Trained using an encoder with history compression techniques, a semantic tokenizer, and rollout distillation from a 0.6B-scale teacher model. The Story of Qwen: Alibaba’s AI Models From 7B to 2.4T An article covering the evolutionary journey and key release history of Alibaba's open-source AI model 'Qwen', from its initial 2023 models to scaling up to 2.4 trillion parameters. Following the unveiling of Tongyi Qianwen in April 2023, Alibaba began its full-scale expansion by open-sourcing Qwen-7B in August in response to Meta's Llama 2. Throughout 2024, Alibaba released three generations—Qwen1.5, Qwen2, and Qwen2.5—in just eight months, establishing itself as a standard open model for developers with Apache 2.0 licensing and context support of up to 128K. It expanded its influence by introducing various specialized models including vision (Qwen-VL), math (Qwen2-Math), audio, and coding (Qwen2.5-Coder), achieving 40 million downloads and inspiring over 50,000 derivative models. Can an Open Model Do Security Research? Cantina’s apex-flash-1 Solves 40 of 60 Held-Out Bug Tasks An introduction and benchmark performance results for apex-flash-1, an open-source 321.3B-parameter security-hardened model specialized for vulnerability research, released by Cantina Security and Yeta Labs. An open-weights model (MIT license) with 321.3B parameters, based on Z.ai's GLM-5.3-Flash and fine-tuned with GRPO reinforcement learning using real-world vulnerability cases. Achieved a 66.7% (40/60) success rate across an evaluation of 60 tasks based on 20 undisclosed vulnerability cases, delivering performance close to Claude Opus 5 High (71.7%). Designed as a specialized worker model that operates under an orchestrator model's directions to perform code analysis, tool use, and exploit development and verification, offering cost efficiency roughly 31 times cheaper than Claude. Meta Muse Explained: What It Is, How It Works, and What It Can Do Meta has launched 'Muse', a personal AI agent that understands user goals and autonomously performs complex tasks 24/7, such as web browsing, payments, and running sub-agents. Meta has unveiled Muse, a personal AI agent that goes beyond simple chatbots to autonomously plan, utilize tools, and monitor tasks. Powered by the Muse Spark 1.3 inference engine, it supports long-term workflow management and the parallel coordination of multiple sub-agents. It provides an isolated Linux virtual machine (VM) environment for each user to securely handle file operations, code execution, and persistent state maintenance. 3 Statsmodels Tricks for Time Series Analysis & Forecasting Introduces three time series analysis and forecasting tips that effectively leverage built-in methods in Python's statsmodels library rather than reimplementing them from scratch. Instead of simple point forecasting, use get_forecast() to easily extract uncertainty information and confidence intervals (conf_int) already calculated by the model. When new observations are added, instead of retraining the entire model (.fit()), efficiently update it using append() or extend() while preserving existing parameters (refit=False). Instead of manually handling seasonal decomposition, forecasting, and recombination, use the STLForecast object to prevent index errors and cumbersome calculations. Fast Models, Slow Evidence: A Paired and Self-Audited Evaluation of System-1 Decision Models for LLM Agent Harnesses This study compares and self-audits the performance and reliability of System-1 models (Laya, Jev)—which process micro-decisions in LLM agent harnesses within a single forward pass—across 11 decision points. The hosted model, Jev, achieved significantly higher accuracy (+10.8 to +46.0%p) than the open-weights model, Laya, in 9 out of 11 evaluated categories. Both models failed to exceed random-chance levels in zero-shot model routing and tied on RAG relevance gating, while Laya exhibited robustness vulnerabilities such as altering its answers 30% of the time when option ordering was reversed. The self-pipeline audit revealed analytical errors that distorted key deployment claims, including the omission of pre-screening costs (a reported 23.9% savings vs. an actual 4.3%) and in-sample threshold distortions. The AI Risk Observatory: What Can We Learn from AI Disclosures in Annual Reports About Societal Resilience? This study analyzes the annual reports of UK-listed companies using a large-scale LLM pipeline to evaluate patterns of corporate AI adoption and risk disclosure, drawing implications for societal resilience. Between 2020 and 2025, the proportion of corporate annual reports mentioning AI risks surged from 2.8% to 41.2%, and mentions of AI adoption jumped from 13.8% to 45.2%, with vendor mentions heavily concentrated among a few major players such as Microsoft. Significant disparities emerged across market sectors and Critical National Infrastructure (CNI) areas, with AIM-listed companies and the energy and data infrastructure sectors lagging substantially behind major markets in AI risk disclosure rates. Actual AI-related harm disclosures totaled only 7 instances across the entire period, and among the 41.2% of companies mentioning risks in 2025, only 4.3% provided substantive disclosures, indicating a lack of qualitative depth in reporting. Keep It CALM: Analyzing the Limits of Global Unsafety in Text-to-Image Generation This paper analyzes the limitations of existing global unsafety removal methods in text-to-image generation models and proposes CALM, a training-free safety guardrail that performs prompt-specific local counterfactual modulation. It geometrically identifies the trade-off between coverage and selectivity in conventional global unsafe subspace removal methods, showing they either fail to fully cover diverse unsafe semantics or inadvertently distort adjacent benign prompts. Instead of uniform global removal, it proposes CALM (Counterfactual Adaptive Local Modulation), which identifies activated unsafe categories on a per-prompt basis and minimally modifies only violating token representations toward a safe direction. Across various evaluations, CALM significantly improved unsafe content suppression performance while preserving generation quality and utility for benign prompts. Choosing Before Acting: Comparative Value Estimation for Long-Horizon Tool-Use Agents This study introduces CITA, a framework that assists long-horizon tool-use agents in comparing and estimating the long-term value of candidate tool calls prior to execution. It points out the limitations in long-horizon interactions where outcome-based rewards suffer from weak credit assignment and step-level rewards incur high supervision costs. It proposes CITA, which trains a Comparative Inference Model (CIM) combining a Bayesian tool graph simulator with LLM-based comparisons to comparatively evaluate alternative calls within the same context prior to actual action. Experiments across three tool-use benchmarks and diverse backbone LLMs validate consistent improvements in Tool F1 scores and final task success rates. DeReAct: Decomposed Reasoning and Acting for Reliable AI Agents A research proposing DeReAct, a modular AI agent architecture that decouples action verification and completion control to address the coupling issues of single LLM policies. Introduced the DeReAct architecture to resolve error propagation and unjustified premature termination caused by conventional ReAct agents' reliance on a single LLM. Decoupled two external gating policies: 'Critic', which verifies actions prior to execution, and 'Context Manager', which reconstructs environment-grounded states to certify task completion. Evaluation results on GAIA and SWE-bench Verified benchmarks demonstrated substantial Pass@1 performance improvements, particularly with relatively lower-performing Brain models, while maintaining evidence-based reliability even as models became more capable. Hybrid Machine Learning-Assisted Raman Spectroscopy with Generative Feature Augmentation for Pharmaceutical Identification A study on a hybrid Raman spectroscopy analysis framework that rapidly and accurately identifies six types of pharmaceutical residues by combining deep learning feature extraction with generative data augmentation techniques Proposes the HyMLRaman framework, which converts Raman spectra into spectral images, extracts 1536-dimensional embeddings using an EfficientNet-B3 backbone, and combines them with traditional machine learning classifiers The hybrid configuration combining EfficientNet-B3 and SVM achieved an accuracy of 96.31% and a macro-F1 score of 96.36%, outperforming standalone CNN baselines Introduces a DDPM-based feature augmentation technique to overcome data scarcity, demonstrating performance improvements in low-data regimes (especially for KNN) and showing the potential for implementing interactive analyzers The Price of Greenwashing: Algorithmic Verification and Market Discipline using Conformal Machine Learning This study leverages conformal machine learning techniques to quantify the gap between corporate self-reported carbon emissions and actual physical emissions, demonstrating the negative impact of greenwashing on a firm's market valuation and profitability. Established a reliable physical carbon emissions baseline by combining US SEC financial fundamentals data with the EPA's facility-level greenhouse gas registry, applying gradient boosted decision trees and Mondrian Conformal Prediction. Developed the Conformal-Weighted Continuous Divergence (CWCD), a novel metric designed to measure discrepancies between self-reported emissions and the algorithmic baseline. Confirmed a market discipline mechanism through a cross-sectional lagged econometric design, showing that a larger algorithmic emissions gap is associated with statistically significant subsequent declines in corporate market valuation (Tobin's Q) and operating return on assets (ROA). State-Space Unlearning for Non-Stationary Bias in Land Surface Forecasting Proposes SSU-LSF, the first machine unlearning framework designed to effectively remove non-stationary bias events absorbed into Mamba-based Structured State-Space Models (SSMs) for land surface forecasting. SSU-LSF was developed to resolve the issue where Mamba-family structured state-space models absorb non-stationary confounding events, such as irrigation surges or dam operation shifts, into their state transition matrices, causing long-term forecasting bias. It applies an EKFac influence function specialized for Mamba state matrices, spectral radius-weighted elbow thresholding, and a Hessian-free projected gradient ascent within a KL-divergence trust region combined with spatial Total Variation (TV) regularization. It reduces GPU costs by 8.4x compared to full retraining while converging in just 3 to 5 epochs, achieving a confounder reduction rate of 0.773 to 0.859 across three benchmarks. Nearest-neighbour baselines for fingerprint prediction from MS/MS spectra under different assumptions This study systematically compares the performance of various nearest-neighbour techniques under different inference assumptions for molecular fingerprint prediction from tandem mass spectrometry (MS/MS) spectra and establishes rigorous baselines. Demonstrated that nearest-neighbour retrieval techniques serve as strong baselines that match or outperform existing deep learning models in molecular fingerprint prediction based on MS/MS spectra. Systematically compared and analyzed various nearest-neighbour variants that differ depending on the assumptions about information available during inference. Aimed to establish baselines that enable more rigorous benchmarking and measurement of research progress by verifying the impact of different assumptions on performance. Overcoming Challenges of Interpretive Structural Modeling with Large Language Models This study investigates utilizing Large Language Models (LLMs) to overcome scalability limitations and inefficiencies in the expert consensus process of Interpretive Structural Modeling (ISM), a multi-criteria decision-making technique. While Interpretive Structural Modeling (ISM) is effective for causal modeling, it is hindered by a cumbersome expert consensus process and difficulty scaling to hundreds of variables. By combining causal graph discovery using LLMs as imperfect experts, various integration methodologies—including pairwise, k-wise, rowwise, and full graph exploration—were compared and evaluated. Experimental results demonstrated that the rowwise and full graph exploration approaches achieved the best performance in causal graph discovery for ISM. HakemBench: A Turkish Benchmark of Typed Decisions HakemBench v1.0, a Turkish benchmark dataset comprising seven domains, has been released to evaluate models' typed decision-making capabilities. HakemBench 1.0 provides 2,346 items and 4,275 questions across seven tracks—fact-check triage, education, guardrails, legal routing, moderation, spam/phishing, and customer support—and is fully open-sourced under CC BY 4.0. It evaluates decision quality (Macro F1), calibration, and selective automation metrics via geometric mean, alongside robustness checks such as option ordering and paraphrase variations. Ground-truth labels were established through cross-voting by a panel of large language models rather than human verification, and evaluation across 16 models showed the top-performing model achieving a score of 0.888. Finding the Move Is Not Winning the Game: XiangqiBench for Closed-Loop Evaluation of LLM Agents This study proposes XiangqiBench, an LLM agent evaluation benchmark utilizing Chinese chess (Xiangqi) to measure the performance gap between static evaluation and actual closed-loop execution. It reveals a significant gap between merely identifying the correct move and the execution ability to achieve a final checkmate amidst the opponent's responses. By evaluating 8,568 multi-turn trajectories across 119 tactical endgame scenarios on 12 frontier LLMs, three major failure modes were identified: the transition gap, consistency gap, and simulation gap. With 32.3% of agent-generated move simulations terminating on illegal moves and 49.3% failing to predict the opposing engine's actual responses, the study emphasizes measuring closed-loop outcomes and reliability rather than static metrics when evaluating agents. Counterexample Generation via Per-Theorem Symbolic Verifiers: When Imitation Hurts and Reinforcement Repairs A study utilizing per-theorem deterministic Python verifiers as reward functions to enhance the counterexample generation capabilities of LLMs and overcome the collapse of imitation learning through reinforcement learning. Construction of the SymCE dataset comprising 4,707 undergraduate-level algebra and real analysis false conjectures accompanied by executable verifiers Counterexample-only supervised fine-tuning (SFT) triggers an imitation trap where true-theorem recognition plummets from 0.27 to 0.00, whereas sparse reward-based RLVR (GRPO) recovers and improves it to 0.66 The developed 4B model outperforms all evaluated 7B open-source math-specialized models and demonstrates generalization performance across major benchmarks including GSM8K, MATH-500, and MMLU-college-math without prompt modifications FinDialogLens: Event Extraction over Multi-Party Dialogue for Missed-Trade Identification in Financial Chatrooms This study proposes FinDialogLens, a hybrid LLM pipeline that applies event extraction techniques to identify missed trades in complex multi-party financial chatrooms. Constructed a hybrid pipeline that utilizes lightweight fine-tuned classifiers to detect RFQ triggers and price/execution metadata, and fills argument roles via an RFQ-level segmentation module and a trade engine. GPT-4o-based FinDialogLens achieves 92.1% accuracy for final price and 94.3% accuracy for trade outcome, outperforming existing whole-chatroom CoT prompting approaches, while achieving comparable performance even with a 3B-scale open-source model. Introduced a difficulty-aware router to distribute RFQs between a low-cost rule-based engine and a high-performance LLM engine, reducing LLM calls by 85% for final price determination and achieving significant cost savings. CUEing User Simulators: Calibrated User Embeddings for Multi-Turn Benchmarking This study proposes the Calibrated User Embeddings (CUE) framework, which guides LLMs to accurately replicate the success rates and failure patterns of real users in multi-turn conversational benchmarks. It was found that while existing user simulators exhibit human-like conversational styles superficially, they lack alignment with the success rates and failure patterns (outcome calibration) observed when real users interact with AI agents. The authors introduce the CUE framework, which encodes observed interaction sessions, samples continuous representations, and decodes them into persona instructions to steer LLM simulators without additional training. Evaluation on τ²-Bench demonstrated that CUE-based simulators reduced simulator-intrinsic errors, accurately replicated real users' failure modes and success rates, and generalized well across diverse domains such as document writing and math tutoring even after being constructed on customer support data. Turning Local MCP Servers into One-Click Extensions for Claude Desktop with MCPB An introduction to using the MCPB format to easily install local MCP servers into Claude Desktop with a single click, just like browser extensions MCPB (.mcpb) is a single zip package format containing a local MCP server and manifest.json Easy installation is possible by double-clicking the file, dragging and dropping it into the window, or using the extension settings menu The installed server runs via stdio in the user's local PC environment, integrating with local files and tools Automating My 35mm Film Scanning Pipeline A build log of automating the time-consuming post-processing of 35mm film photography by combining Python-based algorithms and AI models. Based on past failures with hardware control, scanning was kept manual via VueScan, while post-processing—from negative inversion to dust removal and metadata generation—was automated. Performed inversion while maintaining roll-level color consistency using NumPy-based routines, eliminating reliance on existing manual tools like darktable. Removed dust by combining image analysis with the LaMa inpainting AI model, and built a web gallery with automatically generated search tags using a local Qwen3-VL model. System-Level Ad Blocking on Android Introduces the principles of system-level ad blocking and various implementation methods applicable across the Android operating system and all apps, beyond specific browsers. System-level ad blocking primarily works by altering DNS lookups to block connections to ad servers at the source. Options include ad-blocking DNS services, ad-blocking VPNs, non-root ad-blocking apps that use a local VPN/DNS (such as AdAway), and modifying the hosts file after rooting. Since complete blocking is impossible and privacy risks exist depending on traffic bottlenecks or malicious DNS/VPN operators, choosing a trustworthy service is crucial. Show GN: Alpine-Code - Built a customizable coding agent where you can develop tools for the agent yourself. ... 'Alpine-Code', an open-source AI coding agent featuring a Korean interface and allowing users to expand capabilities by directly developing tools, has been released. As an open-source (MIT) alternative to Codex Desktop and Claude Desktop, users can develop tools directly with Python functions inside the app and add them to the agent loop. It provides a native Korean interface, terminology explanations, approval steps before file modifications and command executions, as well as detailed task logs and cost information. It supports various commercial LLM APIs (Anthropic, OpenAI, Gemini, etc.) alongside local models such as Ollama and LM Studio, as well as terminal CLI environments. Show GN: smooth-stream - A mod that smoothly displays Claude Code responses like a typewriter A mod that displays the streaming responses of Claude Code CLI, which are otherwise output in choppy chunks, with a smooth typewriter effect just like on the web. Smoothly animates and displays Claude Code CLI's streaming output character by character, similar to claude.ai on the web Incrementally increases text length using the ui.render hook and a 33ms timer, implemented to catch up within approximately 0.4 seconds even during sudden text bursts Tracks streaming status via the turn.step hook to prevent unnecessary re-rendering during window resize or for completed messages Sona: one transformer replaced our 15+ candidate generators, pre-ranker and ranker in an A/B test [R] A summary of the development and A/B test results of 'Sona,' a recommendation system at Yandex Music that replaced over 15 existing candidate generators and ranking systems with a single transformer model. Consolidated a complex multi-stage recommendation pipeline (candidate generation, pre-ranking, and ranking) into a single end-to-end transformer named 'Sona.' Applied a History Compression technique to cut attention costs from processing up to 8,192 events, reducing inference costs by approximately half. Statistically significantly improved active user count and total listening time compared to the existing production system in an A/B test on smart speakers, though catalog coverage slightly decreased. I have trained a model to predict my blood sugar (Part 2) [P] A project that deployed a lightweight Transformer model trained on a synthetic data simulator to a mobile app to predict an individual's actual blood sugar levels in a zero-shot manner. Developed an encoder-only Transformer model with 31,251 parameters trained exclusively on synthetic data from a Type 1 Diabetes Mellitus (T1DM) patient simulator. Predicts blood sugar for the next 2 hours, and enables long-term predictions such as 8-hour overnight forecasting and counterfactual reasoning through an autoregressive approach. Validated zero-shot performance using 30 days of real-world data from three CGM devices (Libre 3 plus, Anytime CT5, and Linx) via an ExecuTorch backend-based Android app. Withdrawing an accepted paper before camera-ready due to zero funding? (ACML 2026 / OpenReview) [D] An inquiry regarding the concerns and proper procedures of a researcher seeking to withdraw an accepted paper before submitting the camera-ready version due to a lack of funding for conference registration and travel expenses. Although the paper was accepted to ACML 2026, withdrawing the paper before the camera-ready deadline has become inevitable due to the absence of funding for registration fees and travel expenses. The author is inquiring about the proper procedure—specifically, whether to withdraw directly on the OpenReview platform or to email the Program Chairs first. Concerns are raised regarding potential penalties, such as the risk of being blacklisted alongside co-authors or having a late withdrawal record permanently archived in public records. New forensic evidence supports Egyptian \"retainer sacrifice\" A re-analysis of skull remains from ancient Egypt's First Dynasty, excavated in the 1920s, revealed traces of blunt force trauma, presenting new forensic evidence that supports the practice of retainer sacrifice. According to a new study published in the journal Frontiers in Environmental Archaeology, traces of blunt force trauma were identified in more than one-third of the analyzed skulls. These findings support the 'retainer sacrifice' hypothesis that early pharaohs buried servants and officials alongside them to serve them in the afterlife. Roselyn Campbell, a bioarchaeologist at Purdue University, emphasized the importance of recontextualizing historical skeletal records using advanced forensic techniques, moving beyond colonial perspectives. How I made a paid Mac app in 11 days with Claude – without writing a single line of code The author shares their experience of developing and launching a paid Mac app on the App Store in 11 days through vibe coding using Claude, without writing a single line of code themselves. Completed the Mac app without coding directly through 502 directives, rather than a single magical prompt. Unearthed 2,338 vintage icon assets created 30 years ago and recreated them as a modern Mac app. It took one week from ideation to submitting the finished app to Apple, and it was launched after receiving final approval 4 days later. Akka Tests Spec-Driven AI Delivery Across 65 Open Source Projects Akka experimented with AI-assisted software porting using a specification-driven workflow across 65 open-source projects, analyzing efficiency and performance across different models. Akka invested 99.3 hours and 9.41 billion tokens into AI porting experiments across 65 open-source projects, achieving a reduction in lines of code or performance improvements in 57 out of the 65 ports. Claude Sonnet, a smaller model, took an average of 61 minutes per port compared to 120 minutes for Opus, but Opus consumed about 40% fewer tokens, and it was observed that the smaller model tended to faithfully follow specifications without spontaneous variations. Performance varied significantly by project type: while overall improvements were observed across applications, frameworks, and libraries, performance degradation occurred in the infrastructure and tooling categories. Presentation: Building Reusable Evaluation Frameworks for Agentic AI Products Presents a case study of how Elastic moved away from siloed, ad-hoc AI agent evaluation methods to build a reusable, production-grade unified evaluation framework. Designed an evaluation architecture that balances LLM-as-a-judge approaches with deterministic rules. Bridged the gap between Python-based data science evaluation code and TypeScript production environments, implementing deep tracing. Helps prevent regressions while preserving domain context in complex RAG and cybersecurity workloads. Article: The Platform Engineering Playbook for Production LLMs Introduces practical case studies on significantly reducing hallucination rates and ensuring reliability through platform engineering design when deploying LLMs to production environments, all without modifying the foundation models themselves. Dramatically reduced the production hallucination rate from 15% to 1.5% by introducing intent verification gates and automated retry loops that handle formatting, grounding, and infrastructure errors in real time. Built a system where prompts are stored in a history-based registry rather than hardcoded into code, enabling instant rollbacks and updates at runtime. Prevented the propagation of security vulnerabilities by enforcing a default-deny tool authorization policy at the resource server level rather than relying solely on API gateways. ⚡ Weekly Recap: NetScaler and FortiMail 0-Days, AI Coding Leaks, Spectre v2 and Ransomware Arrests A weekly security trend summary covering zero-day exploitation cases in NetScaler and FortiMail, along with the arrests of key ransomware gang members. Citrix has released a security update for a high-severity memory overflow vulnerability (CVE-2026-88779) in NetScaler ADC and Gateway that was exploited in targeted zero-day attacks. CISA warned that a critical zero-day vulnerability (CVE-2026-104286) in Fortinet FortiMail, which allows unauthenticated attackers to write arbitrary files, is being actively exploited in the wild. Law enforcement authorities arrested two members of the digital extortion gang ShinyHunters and a 16-year-old leader who ran the KillSec ransomware operation. Google Offers Korea-Exclusive 40% Discount on 'AI Pro'... Launches Annual Subscription Promotion Google has launched a promotion offering a 40% discount on 'Google AI Pro' annual subscriptions for Korean users, along with revealing key advanced features and campaign videos. Google is running a promotion through October 31 offering 'Google AI Pro' to new domestic annual subscribers for 174,000 KRW (equivalent to 14,500 KRW per month), a 40% discount from the regular price of 290,000 KRW. The Google AI Pro plan includes 5TB of storage along with premium features such as 'Deep Research', personal AI agents, Workspace integration, 'Antigravity', and 'Gemini Omni'. Starting on the 9th, Google will discontinue providing the 'Gemini Pro' model in free and select lower-tier plans, while differentiating the AI Pro plan by enhancing in-depth reasoning capabilities such as 'Deep Think'. Claude Authors 36 Paper Manuscripts Across 18 Fields: Anthropic Highlights AI in Scientific Research Harvard University Professor Matthew Schwartz presented a new collaborative model for AI-driven scientific research by drafting 36 paper manuscripts across 18 fields using Claude and an open-source harness system called 'BootLoops'. To address the 'impedance mismatch' between AI and active scientific research, he defined 'Claude-shaped problems' optimized for LLM strengths and built the open-source harness system 'BootLoops'. By extending Feynman diagram computational tools from physics to diverse disciplines such as population genetics, ecology, economics, and linguistics, long-standing unsolved equations were solved and large-scale validations were performed. By compensating for AI hallucinations and the lack of value judgment with guidance and direction from human researchers, it demonstrated that AI serves not as a replacement for scientists, but as a collaborator filling gaps in knowledge. Anthropic Runs 'Voice Interviewer' Through October 6, Combining Transcript Releases with Voice Training Data Collection Anthropic is expanding operations of its conversational AI research agent 'Anthropic Interviewer' to publish user interview transcripts and collect data for training voice models. Anthropic launched a large-scale qualitative interview study lasting 15 minutes targeting general Claude and Claude Code users. The scope of data disclosure was expanded to allow respondents to directly choose whether to publicly release their full anonymized transcripts after completing the interview. While consent is concurrently collected for voice data training to improve speech recognition and response performance, privacy concerns are emerging regarding the potential re-identification of anonymized data. KAIST Develops 'CONDA' Technology to Maintain Search Accuracy Despite Data Changes KAIST researchers have developed 'CONDA', a dynamic vector search technology that enhances search accuracy and speed by preserving path connectivity even during frequent additions and deletions of data. Developed the dynamic vector search technology 'CONDA' to solve the 'connectivity collapse' problem where paths in the vector search network are broken as data continuously changes Prevented path isolation and reduced update costs through three core technologies: Connectivity-aware Route Navigation Graph (CRNG), new data connection reinforcement, and efficient Lazy Deletion Achieved up to 24.5% higher search accuracy and up to 1.90x faster processing speed compared to existing methods in large-scale experiments with 100 million data points; scheduled for commercialization in Q4 of this year via integration into Graphy's 'AkashicDB' GPT-6 Astra Deciphers 217-Year-Old Napoleonic Cipher in Just 6 Hours OpenAI's GPT-6 Astra has succeeded in autonomously deciphering a 217-year-old unsolved Napoleonic-era military cipher in just 6 hours. SentinelOne engineer Carter Church deciphered an 1809 French military cipher in approximately 6 hours by feeding GPT-6 Astra only a single image and a prompt. Astra independently completed the entire workflow, including symbol transcription, structural analysis, autonomous search and cross-referencing of external historical literature, and running its own simulated annealing solver. Through this decryption, severed sentences from the 'Correspondence of Napoleon I' were restored, and Tomokiyo Satoshi, operator of Cryptiana, officially confirmed the decryption results."